// ═══ IO-BOUND: concurrency shines (many threads, few cores) ═══
// 100 HTTP calls, each takes 200ms waiting for response.
// Sequential: 100 × 200ms = 20 seconds!
// Concurrent (100 threads): all 100 calls in parallel → ~200ms total!
// Thread count: 100 threads on 4 cores → works great!
// While thread-1 waits for HTTP response, thread-2 uses the CPU.
ExecutorService ioPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(100);  // many threads!
List<Future<String>> futures = new ArrayList<>();
for (String url : urls) {
    futures.add(ioPool.submit(() -> httpGet(url)));  // 200ms IO wait each
}
// All 100 calls complete in ~200ms, not 20 seconds!

// ═══ CPU-BOUND: parallelism needed (cores threads) ═══
// Encrypt 1M records, each takes 1ms CPU.
// Sequential: 1M × 1ms = 1000 seconds!
// Parallel (4 cores): 1M / 4 = 250K per core → ~250 seconds!
// Thread count: 4 threads on 4 cores → optimal!
// More threads = context switching overhead > benefit!
ExecutorService cpuPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(
    Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());  // = number of cores!
// parallelStream() also uses ForkJoinPool (cores threads)
long sum = data.parallelStream().mapToLong(this::encrypt).sum();

// ═══ THREAD COUNT GUIDELINES ═══
// IO-bound:  threads = cores × (1 + waitTime/computeTime)
//            e.g., 4 cores, 200ms wait, 10ms compute → 4 × 21 = 84 threads
// CPU-bound: threads = cores (or cores + 1 for scheduling margin)
//            e.g., 4 cores → 4 threads
// Mixed:     separate pools! IO pool (many threads) + CPU pool (cores threads)

// ═══ EXAMPLE: BOTH in banking ═══
// IO-bound: 200 concurrent database queries → 200-thread pool
// CPU-bound: encrypt 100K records → 4-thread pool (4 cores)
// DON'T: use one pool for both → IO tasks starve CPU tasks (or vice versa)
